import{_ as o,o as t,c as n,a2 as s}from"./chunks/framework.BUOFcz_J.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Connecting your own Agent with one prompt","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"records/agent-connect-episode.md","filePath":"records/agent-connect-episode.md"}'),a={name:"records/agent-connect-episode.md"};function i(r,e,d,c,h,l){return t(),n("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s('<div class="warning custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">Historical record</p><p>This page records what happened on a date, with its observed failures and limits. It is not a requirement for the current implementation. Current behaviour is in <a href="/Rho/docs/reference/status">Status</a>.</p></div><h1 id="connecting-your-own-agent-with-one-prompt" tabindex="-1">Connecting your own Agent with one prompt <a class="header-anchor" href="#connecting-your-own-agent-with-one-prompt" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Connecting your own Agent with one prompt&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Date: 2026-11-06. Source changes after Rho 0.5.5; package publication and installation are separate. This episode changes Rho only. Core and Plugins are unchanged.</p><blockquote><p>Superseded in part on 2026-11-07: the Agent page now has Conversations and Agents; the setup prompt sits under Agents, and New conversation settings became the Agents list (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/session-continuity-episode">session continuity</a>).</p></blockquote><h2 id="question-and-observed-problem" tabindex="-1">Question and observed problem <a class="header-anchor" href="#question-and-observed-problem" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Question and observed problem&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The person could not tell whether Agents or Connections was where to connect their own Agent. Agents configured conversations started inside Rho; Connections offered brand-specific Claude Code and Codex buttons beside an ACP relay option. Those choices made a generic MCP capability look like a two-client restriction.</p><p>The question was whether the person&#39;s own Agent could configure itself from one copied prompt, without another brand-specific form in Rho.</p><h2 id="what-changed" tabindex="-1">What changed <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-changed" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What changed&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The Agent page has Conversations and Connect your agent. The default Agent and model choices now sit under New conversation settings in Conversations and explain that they apply inside Rho. Connect your agent leads with a copyable request and a selectable full prompt. Existing grants appear under their actual session names, with waiting, verified last call, refusal or ended access states.</p><p>The local-browser/operator-only <code>session.instructions</code> reads the running launcher&#39;s program and argument array and exact project identity. It includes no credential contents, starts nothing and grants no access. The Agent runs the existing grant CLI, chooses its own stable session name and registers the returned stdio MCP entry using its own client. The added <code>--project-id</code> selects a registered project without reopening a guessed path, including a folder selected outside the service root. The old named CLI shortcuts remain compatible.</p><p>Repeated setup with the same scope keeps its valid credential. Changed methods, expired access or a stale address require a fresh one. Granting now emits a browser invalidation: the first browser run exposed a grant that existed but was invisible until another event or reload. Refused calls remain visible and a successful retry uses the same connection.</p><h2 id="real-runs-and-corrections" tabindex="-1">Real runs and corrections <a class="header-anchor" href="#real-runs-and-corrections" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Real runs and corrections&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>node scripts/agent-connect-episode.mjs</code> creates a disposable Unicode/spaced project, an isolated Claude configuration directory and a real service without Core. Chrome copies the prompt through the real clipboard. The Agent receives that exact prompt, plus the boundary that its configuration belongs in the disposable environment. Existing model credentials travel only through the process environment.</p><p>Two initial harness failures were corrected: reload returned to the project&#39;s Overview rather than its Agent page, and a variadic CLI option consumed the prompt. Neither was evidence of an Agent configuring itself.</p><p>The next run configured a shared project MCP entry. Claude&#39;s own approval and the harness&#39;s disabled settings sources kept the fresh client from loading it. The Agent had separately made a direct stdio probe, and the original assertion wrongly counted that earlier receipt as the new client&#39;s success. Reading both replies invalidated that run (<code>work/agent-connect/1793992731271/report.json</code>, review field). A direct server probe proves the server answers, not that the client&#39;s tools work.</p><p>The prompt now requests private local configuration outside version-controlled files and checks <code>scopeHint.projectId</code>, which <code>rho_index</code> actually returns. The harness uses the isolated client&#39;s normal settings sources. Verification requires both a fresh native <code>mcp__…__rho_index</code> tool-use event and a new successful server receipt after the second session starts; an earlier probe cannot satisfy it. A further run loaded the tools but the harness’s wildcard permission did not authorize them in Claude’s <code>dontAsk</code> mode. The harness now names the exact <code>rho_index</code> tool it is testing and requires zero permission denials; it changes no user configuration or native permission policy.</p><p>The final run (<code>work/agent-connect/1793993054868</code>) passed: Claude Code 2.1.319 configured its own private local entry in 27.1 seconds, then a fresh client called <code>mcp__rho-study__rho_index</code> in 8.5 seconds. The returned project ID matched, both sessions had zero permission denials, and the first session honestly reported that its own tools needed a new session. No project configuration file was written. The harness asks only for the ID that <code>rho_index</code> supplies; an earlier request for a folder path led to additional, ungranted diagnostic calls and was narrowed.</p><h2 id="checks-and-screen-review" tabindex="-1">Checks and screen review <a class="header-anchor" href="#checks-and-screen-review" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Checks and screen review&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><code>npm run build</code>: service and WebUI compiled. The existing large-bundle and mixed static/dynamic import warnings remain.</li><li><code>npm run check:contracts</code> and <code>npm run test:contracts</code>: generated schemas/types match the implementation; 11 contract checks pass.</li><li>The Agent connection, CLI refusals, browser, MCP and progressive-disclosure tests: 30 pass with the existing Core debug binary explicitly selected. The first run lacked that binary for its managed MCP case and omitted Origin in a new pairing test; both failures are retained in the local logs.</li><li><code>npm run acceptance:agent-page</code>: settings, conversations, export, trash, generic grants, refused calls and recovery pass in Chrome. Its conversation peers are controlled fixtures, not model trials.</li><li>The connection episode checks a real clipboard, a failed instructions request and retry, denied clipboard access with selectable text, Unicode/spaced paths, and the setup screens at 1440, 1024, 768 and 390 px. The screenshots show the prompt before connection details, no brand picker and no horizontal overflow; the narrow layout wraps the copy action and keeps the full prompt accessible.</li><li><code>npm run docs:assemble</code>, <code>npm run docs:check</code> and <code>npm run docs:build</code>: 58 pages and 324 rewritten links; changed pages rendered and read, with their new anchors resolving. The initial check correctly required regenerating navigation after the new episode was added.</li></ul><p>The private run directories and raw model outputs stay in ignored <code>work/</code>. No credentials or private service addresses are source evidence. Setup with another client, remote/HTTP-only MCP clients, Windows and independent usability remain unverified by this episode. This work adds no new Agent execution adapter and does not move an external conversation&#39;s input into Rho.</p><h2 id="one-list-and-ready-entries" tabindex="-1">One list and ready entries <a class="header-anchor" href="#one-list-and-ready-entries" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;One list and ready entries&quot;">​</a></h2><p>2026-11-07, after Rho 0.5.6. The person gave the setup prompt to DeepSeek Harness, in its Desktop app on the PA14 project. Harness connected itself, and <code>rho_index</code> answered in the same session, but the whole connection took 8 min 52 s. The Agent then broke its own run down: Rho&#39;s part was about 0.3 s (grant 0.18 s, MCP start 0.19 s, <code>rho_index</code> 0.08 s). It spent 194.5 s looking for how Harness adds an MCP server and 87.8 s reading four package READMEs to find it. Harness has no <code>dsh mcp</code> command; the entry belongs in the profile patch <code>~/.dsh/profiles/desktop/cordis.patch.yml</code>, which reloads within seconds. Its own suggestion was a ready <code>dsh</code> entry in Rho&#39;s reply.</p><p>On the Agents tab, the connected window appeared only in a separate &quot;Agent windows connected&quot; list below the setup prompt, with no mark. OpenCode was listed as <code>opencode</code> with an initial.</p><p>What changed:</p><ul><li><strong>Ready entries.</strong> The grant reply now also gives <code>codex</code>, <code>opencode</code> (the file and the entry to merge) and <code>dsh</code> (the profile patch and the entry to append), beside <code>mcp</code> and <code>claude</code>. The setup prompt tells an Agent to use the entry for its client, and the Rho skill&#39;s connection guide describes the OpenCode and Harness mechanisms.</li><li><strong>One list.</strong> The Agents tab lists every Agent of the project in one row design: those Rho starts, and those connected from their own windows. A window sits under the Agent it belongs to, with its state, its grant and its last call. The setup prompt follows the list.</li><li><strong>Whose window.</strong> The MCP bridge (Agent 0.4.31) sends its client&#39;s own <code>clientInfo</code> name and version. Rho keeps it with the grant and uses it to place the window and name it. When a bridge does not send it yet, the session name the Agent chose decides.</li><li><strong>Marks.</strong> OpenCode has its own mark, drawn from its brand files in the theme&#39;s ink. Names containing <code>dsh</code> take the DeepSeek mark and the name DeepSeek Harness. An Agent with no known maker keeps its initial.</li></ul><p>Runs after the change:</p><ul><li><strong>OpenCode setup again.</strong> The setup prompt was given to OpenCode 1.19.5 in a disposable project, with the same model and no auto-approval. It used the <code>opencode</code> entry and finished in 90 s instead of 213 s, with 9 tool calls. The new session called <code>rho_index</code> in 16 s, and Rho recorded its client as <code>opencode 1.19.5</code>.</li><li><strong>Agents list.</strong> With an OpenCode window and a Harness-named window, the list showed OpenCode&#39;s row with its window beneath it, and a DeepSeek Harness row with its window. There was no overflow or page error at 1440 or 390 px.</li><li><strong>The person&#39;s own 47101 page.</strong> It serves the rebuilt pages from disk. With its earlier service still running, it showed <code>dsh-deepseek-agent</code> as DeepSeek Harness and a WorkBuddy window under its initial.</li><li><strong>Hermes Agent.</strong> It connected itself to PA14 as <code>hermes-agent-yonghe</code> and is listed under its initial. Its code leaves the Python MCP SDK&#39;s default <code>clientInfo</code>, <code>mcp</code> 0.1.0, which would have named its window <code>mcp</code>. A library&#39;s default name is now ignored, and the session name the Agent chose names the window. The Agent page acceptance sends <code>mcp/0.1.0</code> and checks this.</li><li><strong>Marks from a plugin.</strong> Rho drew four marks of its own, so Hermes and WorkBuddy showed initials. The person supplied a collection of 54 Agent and maker icons, each compared with its product&#39;s site. It is now the Agent icons plugin in Rho-plugins, which has 62 entries: Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode and Nous Research (for Hermes) were added from the same lobehub version. A disposable service had windows named as on the PA14 page. It showed Hermes Agent, WorkBuddy, DeepSeek Harness and OpenCode with their marks, and My-research-agent with its initial, at 1440 and 390 px.</li></ul><p>Checks: the full Rho suite passed (315, none failed, 2 skipped). The connection test now also covers the ready entries, the absence of credentials in the reply, a stored client name, and an unprintable name being ignored. 33 Agent plugin tests, 4 Agent icons tests and the Agent page acceptance in Chrome passed.</p><p>Limits:</p><ul><li>Harness was not run again with the <code>dsh</code> entry. Only the person&#39;s own Desktop run is measured, and the entry assumes the <code>desktop</code> profile.</li><li>What name Harness&#39;s MCP client sends was not observed.</li><li>An Agent is still known only by words in its name. One whose name has no word in the catalog shows its initial, and an Agent cannot give its own icon.</li><li>The person&#39;s 47101 service runs the earlier server until it is restarted. It does not yet keep client names or give ready entries.</li></ul><h2 id="one-command" tabindex="-1">One command <a class="header-anchor" href="#one-command" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;One command&quot;">​</a></h2><p>2026-11-07. The person found the setup prompt too complicated, even with Rho installed and running. The prompt had six paragraphs:</p><ul><li>configuration rules;</li><li>an argument array with the paths of Node, Rho&#39;s <code>cli.js</code> and the operator connection file;</li><li>the characters a session name may use;</li><li>how to read the JSON reply, and the ready entries;</li><li>how to verify the connection.</li></ul><p>All of it was for the Agent, but the person copied and read it.</p><p>The prompt now names the project and gives one command:</p><blockquote><p>Connect yourself to my Rho project &quot;PA14 quorum sensing&quot; so we can work on it together.</p><p>Rho is already running on this machine. Run this command with a short, stable name for yourself in place of NAME, and follow its reply:</p><p><code>rho connect &quot;/Users/me/Rho/projects/PA14 quorum sensing&quot; --as NAME</code></p></blockquote><p><code>rho connect</code> finds the project holding that folder in the Rho running for the state folder, grants it under that name, and replies with the rest. The prompt names <code>--state-dir</code> only for a state folder other than <code>~/Rho/state</code>. It uses Node and <code>cli.js</code> only when the first <code>rho</code> on the service&#39;s PATH is not this installation. A missing name, <code>NAME</code> itself, a folder that is not a project and a stopped Rho are refused, with what to do next.</p><p>Runs with OpenCode 1.19.5, in a disposable project. As before, the trials used its own model and no auto-approval, with <code>rho</code> on PATH and a disposable state folder:</p><ul><li><strong>The trial&#39;s <code>rho</code> was not executable.</strong> The first run used a link to the worktree&#39;s <code>dist/cli.js</code>, which tsc leaves without the execute bit; npm sets it on installation. OpenCode looked for another <code>rho</code> and found Rho-core&#39;s old debug binary, which has no <code>connect</code>.</li><li><strong>Connected too early.</strong> With <code>rho</code> executable, <code>rho connect</code> answered at 2 s. OpenCode wrote the project&#39;s <code>.opencode/opencode.json</code> and finished in 62 s with 7 tool calls. It then started the MCP server itself over stdio and reported &quot;connected&quot;. It had also passed <code>scopeHint</code> to <code>rho_index</code> as an argument. The reply said both less clearly than the old prompt had.</li><li><strong>Reply clarified.</strong> Step 2 now says to stop and ask for a new session when the client loads servers only at start. Step 3 says to call <code>rho_index</code> with no arguments. A paragraph says that starting the server yourself does not connect your client. In a fresh project, OpenCode configured itself in 32 s with 5 tool calls. It said that it needed a new session and did not claim a connection. The new session called <code>rho-oc-study3_rho_index</code> through its own tools in 4 s, with the right project, and Rho recorded <code>opencode 1.19.5</code>. Setup had taken 213 s with the first prompt and 90 s with ready entries.</li></ul><p>OpenCode twice stopped after its own <code>init</code> with no output, before any model call. Once, another OpenCode started on the same machine at the same time. The other time was the first start after OpenCode had installed <code>@opencode-ai/plugin</code> into the new <code>.opencode</code> folder: it ran no MCP server and used no CPU. The next start in each case worked. This is the stall the <a href="/Rho/docs/records/opencode-episode#observation-through-opencodes-own-plugin">OpenCode episode</a> saw after adding a plugin.</p><p><strong>A start from source.</strong> The person then copied the prompt from their own Rho. That Rho had been started from source with its own state folder, with no <code>rho</code> on PATH. The command still began with Node and <code>cli.js</code> and ended with <code>--state-dir</code>. Two changes followed:</p><ul><li><strong>Running notes.</strong> Each Rho started from the CLI now leaves a note in <code>~/Rho/running/</code> with its state folder, address and process ID, and no credential. Without <code>--state-dir</code>, <code>rho connect</code> asks the running Rhos which of them holds the folder. The prompt names <code>--state-dir</code> only for a Rho with no note.</li><li><strong>Executable <code>cli.js</code>.</strong> The build now marks <code>dist/cli.js</code> executable. tsc had left it without the execute bit after every build, so a checkout linked onto PATH would not run, as in the first OpenCode run.</li></ul><p>This checkout&#39;s <code>rho</code> was then linked into the person&#39;s npm <code>bin</code> folder. A Rho started with <code>rho</code> on a disposable state gave the prompt command <code>rho connect /private/tmp/rho-e2e/projects/study --as NAME</code>. Run from the project folder with an empty home, the command connected. After <code>rho --stop</code>, the note was gone and the command said that Rho is not running.</p><p>Checks: the connection test covers the prompt&#39;s three lines and its command. It also covers the refusals, the reply&#39;s steps, <code>--json</code>, the absence of credentials and finding a noted Rho without its state folder. The test that gives a fake Agent 0.4 s failed once in four runs on its own on this machine; it is unchanged.</p><p>Limits:</p><ul><li>Only OpenCode was run.</li><li>The prompt&#39;s <code>rho</code> is found on the service&#39;s PATH. An Agent app started without that PATH, such as some desktop apps, would not find it. The OpenCode run shows that the Agent then searches for it.</li><li>A source checkout shows Node and <code>cli.js</code> until its <code>rho</code> is linked onto PATH.</li></ul>',46)])])}const g=o(a,[["render",i]]);export{u as __pageData,g as default};
